```java
interface Transaction {
    TransactionResult execute(BankService bank);
    TransactionResult undo(BankService bank);
    String describe(); // for the audit log
}

class WithdrawTransaction implements Transaction {
    private final String accountId;
    private final long amountCents;
    private final String idempotencyKey;

    public TransactionResult execute(BankService bank) {
        return bank.debit(accountId, amountCents, idempotencyKey);
    }
    public TransactionResult undo(BankService bank) {
        return bank.credit(accountId, amountCents, idempotencyKey + "-undo");
    }
    public String describe() {
        return "Withdraw " + amountCents + " from " + accountId;
    }
}
```

Making each transaction type a `Transaction` object with `execute`/`undo`/`describe` is the
Command pattern earning its keep: the ATM can log every `describe()` for an audit trail, and
reversing a transaction is a defined operation instead of a manual database fix.

```java
class BankService {
    private final Set<String> processedKeys = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();

    TransactionResult debit(String accountId, long amountCents, String idempotencyKey) {
        if (!processedKeys.add(idempotencyKey)) {
            return TransactionResult.alreadyProcessed(idempotencyKey);
        }
        return applyDebit(accountId, amountCents); // real debit against account storage
    }
}
```

The key is generated once per transaction attempt on the ATM side and resent unchanged on every
retry. `processedKeys.add()` returning false on the second call is the whole mechanism: a retry
after a timeout is recognized as a duplicate and short-circuited to the original result, not
re-executed.
